--- Finding: two records compared with `eq`/`ne` where one side is held by
--- reference do not build compiled. The interpreter prints 1, then P[name = 'b'];
--- the compiled program does not build:
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types   (`if left != k`, k: &P lent, left: P)
---     error[E0277]: can't compare `&P` with `P`   (`if p != k`, p a loop's item)
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused
---     what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- Expected: 1 and P[name = 'b'] in both modes. Shape 1: a record parameter the
--- body only reads is lent (`k: &P`) and compared with a local record. Shape 2: a
--- statement loop's item, borrowed from the list it walks, compared with a
--- record. (A record of ints only is passed by value, so shape 1 needs a field
--- that is not Copy, here text; shape 2 shows with ints too.) Found writing the
--- Kirkpatrick–Seidel algorithm (`if left ne k` in its CONNECT step); worked
--- around by comparing the fields (`same[a, b]` = a.x eq b.x and a.y eq b.y).

struct P [name]

fn differs[k, ps]
    left = ps[1]
    if left ne k
        return 1
    return 0

log[differs[P['a'], [P['a'], P['b']]]]

k = P['a']
for p in [P['a'], P['b']]
    if p ne k
        log[p]
